============ ex00 ===========

Exercise 00
ft_putchar
Turn-in directory: ex00/
Files to turn in: ft_putchar.c
Allowed functions: write
• Write a function that displays the character passed as a parameter.
• The function should be prototyped as follows:
  void ft_putchar(char c);
To display the character, you must use the write function as follows:
  write(1, &c, 1);

*************
in C, a character usually use 'A', attention, single quote
char, is a data type, is to save a character
for example:
  char c
    create a thing named c, to save a character
    if c = 'A'

parameter:
  with the example given in ex00, void ft_putchar(char c)
  here the part char c is a parameter
  can understand it as: use char c to run ft_putchar
  here char: type, c: name
  the type of the character: a parameter named as c
  so ft_putchar('A'); can be understood as:
    'A' -> entered as parameter c 
    now what c saved is 'A'
    and ft_putchar can run
char c here is not a value, it's a parameter, it says that the function need to receive a value in type char, and inside this function we call this value as c

definition:
  ft_putchar(char c)
  here char c is the parameter
use:
  ft_putchar('A')
  here 'A' is the argument/value

we can separete the example here in 3 parts
  example in ex00: void ft_putchar(char c);
part 1: void
  void: what to return
part 2: ft_putchar
  ft_putchar: the function name
part 3: (char c)
  (char c): what to receive

void: when the function finished its work, don't return a value
void returns nothing

write itself is a function
write(part1, part2, part3);
example:
write(1, &c, 1);

part1: write it to where -> standard output, which is terminal
  in the example, the first 1 here means file descriptor 1=standard output(stdout)
  0 -> standard input (stdin)
  1 -> standard output (stdout)
  2 -> standard error (stderr)
  stdout and stderr usually show in terminal as well, just two different output streams
part2: where is the data -> the memory address where character c is
part3: how many to write -> 1 byte in the example

what does & mean here?
  c = value
  &c = address of c
why write needs &c?
  write needs to know, where is the data that you wants me to output
  &c means, go to the memory address of c to find the data

'K' -> character -> char -> what we need in this ex00
"K" -> string -> don't need it now

function prototype vs. function definition vs. function body
----------
{
  ...
}
----------
the thing between ---- is the function body

CPU can't execute the C source code directly, since C source code is more readable for human beings, not for the computer
so we need a compiler to make the C source code readable for the computer, turn the C source code to readable machine code
in 42 Moulinette will use cc -Wall -Wextra -Werror to compile my codes
cc: command to use C compiler

1. why use write, we need #include <unistd.h>?
because write is not a function created by me, it's a function offered by the system itself
how a compiler know what write is?
with this: #include <unistd.h>
unistd.h -> a header file, which include some statement that the compiler needs to know, including the statement of write
#include <unistd.h> this is saying: hey Compiler, next I will use something defiend in unistd.h, which include write.

2. why missing ; in 
  void ft_putchar(char c) 
  {
    ...
  }
because it is defining function definition
{ } is telling C that funciton body starts here and ends here

and void ft_putchar(char c); this is a function prototype, telling that: hey, here is a function

3. int main(void)
int here is integer type
main is a function as well, main is the entry function when C programme starts executing
when I run a C program, the system starts from main()
so here:
  int main(void)
  int: return int
  main: function name
  void: do not receive parameter
here the meaning of void is different from the previous void
in the previous void, it means: function returns nothing
  example: void ft_putchar(char c);
and inside int main(void), it means function takes no parameters
  example: int main(void)
    main is a function, this function does not receive parameter, and it will return a integer
    in main, 0 usually means the program ends normally/successfully

summary:
  void function(...) : do not return value
  function(void) : do not receive parameter

So in total:
1. we need to create a function ft_putchar(char c) and use write funciton to display the character
2. to use write, we need to add the header
3. but ex00 only needs to turn in the function, not the program, so don't turn the program in

So we also separete ex00 in severl parts:
1. we create the function itself
void ft_putchar(char c)
{
  write(1, &c, 1);
}

2. then add the header to C source code so that we can use write, because write is a function by default in C
#include <unistd.h>
void ft_putchar(char c)
{
  write(1, &c, 1);
}

3. then we check the function with a program and let it run, to see if the function runs well
int main(void)
{
  ft_putchar('K');
  return(0);
}

we compile the C source code into an executable so the computer can run it.
main() is let the test code to be a entry of a complete program; compiler itself is not used to add main function

although with ex00, we don't need to turn in something compiled, to check it, we need to compile it and test it
to check the funciton, there's a another way without using main 

to compile, we use
cc -Wall -Wextra -Werror ft_putcharcheck.c

cc: C compiler
-Wall: open common warnings
-Wextra: open more warnings
-Werror: take warning as error
ft_putcharcheck.c: the C source file that needs compile

if it returns nothing and comes back to the home, good thing

ls
we can see something like a.out
why a.out?
  since I didn't tell cc what is the executable called, by default, it generates a.out
  if we want to name the output, we can use:
    cc -Wall -Wextra -Werror ft_putcharcheck.c -o check
  the output will be named as check
then ./a.out
we can see K%
or check it with ./a.out | cat -e
K% again

we can also check it with:
cc -Wall -Wextra -Werror -c ft_putchar.c
here -c is new, means compile source code, but not to link it as a complete executable
if there is no error, it will generate ft_putchar.o, this is an object file, just for test, need to be deleted later


